{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 15, :comments ["<b>And the priest shall bring it near to the altar</b> — This bringing near is the act of bringing up: he brings the bird up to the altar, and there he performs melikhah (the pinching off of the head), for melikhah is performed only on the top of the altar. Because of this, the Sages expounded (Zevaḥim 65a): \"The priest\" — for would it enter one's mind that a non-priest brings an offering near the altar? If so, why is it stated \"the priest... and he shall perform melikhah\"? This teaches that melikhah may be performed only by the priest himself. <b>And he shall perform melikhah and burn it and drain out its blood</b> — It is possible to say thus: since he burns it, he drains its blood. Rather, just as the burning of the head is done by itself and the body by itself, so too the melikhah of the head is done by itself and the body by itself. But the plain sense of the verse is inverted: \"and he shall perform melikhah and burn it,\" and before the burning its blood has already been drained out — this is the language of Rashi (Rashi on Leviticus 1:15). And it is impossible to say that he performs melikhah on the head and burns it, and afterward drains the blood of the body on the wall of the altar and burns it, because limbs are never offered in any sacrifice except after the presentation of the blood, for \"it is the blood that makes atonement through the soul\" in all the sacrifices. Therefore they expounded thus: the verse comes only to draw an analogy between melikhah and the burning — in both of them, the head is by itself and the body is by itself. But the plain sense of the verse — \"and he shall perform melikhah on its head to burn it on the altar\" — teaches about the melikhah that it must be performed so as to burn the head at the same time as he burns the body, as it says (Leviticus 1:17), \"and the priest shall burn it,\" for it is the way of the verse in all the sacrifices to speak thus, as I explained (Ramban on Leviticus 1:6) regarding the sections of the burnt offering. But here (Leviticus 1:17) it says \"and the priest shall burn it\" and does not say \"and he shall burn all of it,\" because there are two acts of burning: he burns the head and removes the crop and splits it, and he burns the body, as we learned in Zevaḥim (ibid.)."], :book :leviticus, :voice :ramban, :lang :en, :chapter 1, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T03:49:39.660575774Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}